Unix utility 'dmesg'

The Unix utility 'dmesg' displays the message history the Hexagon kernel has kept since
boot. The log is read straight from the kernel, through its own diagnostic interface, and
holds what the kernel itself recorded: device detection, mounts and unmounts, errors and
any other message a component chose to register.

The output is shown one screen at a time. When a screen fills, a '-- More --' prompt waits
at the bottom row: any key shows the next screen, and 'q' leaves the listing. The number of
lines per screen comes from the console in use, so a change of resolution is accounted for.

Messages sent by utilities through 'syslogd' are not part of this listing, which carries
only what the kernel wrote.

USAGE:

dmesg

No additional parameters are required.

For more information, enter 'dmesg ?' or 'dmesg --help'.

HISTORY

This command appeared in the Hexagonix Mineru development branch (2026).

